1
00:00:00,032 --> 00:00:04,310
Now, as a 3rd step, we now want to install

2
00:00:04,663 --> 00:00:06,553
and configure a firewall

3
00:00:07,387 --> 00:00:10,143
to be able to control which kind of traffic

4
00:00:10,512 --> 00:00:14,277
can actually reach our VPS. Now, I will say

5
00:00:14,293 --> 00:00:17,210
that many providers, including Hetzner,

6
00:00:18,043 --> 00:00:19,998
make it very easy for you to set up a

7
00:00:19,998 --> 00:00:20,511
firewall.

8
00:00:21,408 --> 00:00:23,235
On Hetzner, for example, if you click on

9
00:00:23,267 --> 00:00:25,238
your server, you can manage it and there is

10
00:00:25,334 --> 00:00:26,119
a firewalls.

11
00:00:26,712 --> 00:00:27,145
Point here.

12
00:00:28,059 --> 00:00:29,935
And it's similar for EC2.

13
00:00:30,704 --> 00:00:32,853
If you launch an EC2 instance already,

14
00:00:33,029 --> 00:00:35,691
actually, you can create a so-called

15
00:00:35,691 --> 00:00:38,111
security group, which is in the end also a

16
00:00:38,111 --> 00:00:41,366
kind of firewall, which as a default already

17
00:00:41,607 --> 00:00:45,022
only allows SSH traffic but has no other

18
00:00:45,054 --> 00:00:47,186
rules. You can tick some here and you can

19
00:00:47,378 --> 00:00:49,960
add more once you finished setting it up.

20
00:00:51,402 --> 00:00:53,871
You can also manage your security groups

21
00:00:53,952 --> 00:00:55,491
after you created them.

22
00:00:56,292 --> 00:00:58,216
And in such a security group,

23
00:00:58,761 --> 00:01:01,005
you can edit your inbound rules, for

24
00:01:01,005 --> 00:01:04,035
example, and add new rules to control which

25
00:01:04,131 --> 00:01:06,680
kind of traffic on which port is able to

26
00:01:06,712 --> 00:01:09,261
reach the server technically. So that's a

27
00:01:09,357 --> 00:01:12,050
great security mechanism. And Hetzner, as

28
00:01:12,146 --> 00:01:14,150
mentioned, has something similar. You could

29
00:01:14,150 --> 00:01:16,458
create a firewall here and could control

30
00:01:16,474 --> 00:01:19,039
which kind of traffic can reach it. And I'll

31
00:01:19,039 --> 00:01:21,379
get back to that because using that

32
00:01:21,379 --> 00:01:23,816
provider, provided firewall

33
00:01:24,649 --> 00:01:26,989
is actually a good idea. But I'm also

34
00:01:26,989 --> 00:01:29,264
showing you the approach that does not rely

35
00:01:29,392 --> 00:01:31,411
on it because you may not be using a

36
00:01:31,475 --> 00:01:33,318
provider that offers this feature.

37
00:01:34,504 --> 00:01:38,286
In that case, you may wanna use the UFW

38
00:01:38,671 --> 00:01:40,530
firewall, which should already be

39
00:01:40,530 --> 00:01:42,228
pre-installed on Ubuntu.

40
00:01:42,757 --> 00:01:44,888
If it's not installed, you can use your

41
00:01:45,000 --> 00:01:49,151
package manager, apt on Ubuntu, and install

42
00:01:49,808 --> 00:01:50,400
ufw.

43
00:01:51,586 --> 00:01:53,765
Now, that will fail if you're trying to do

44
00:01:53,765 --> 00:01:56,649
it as a user because installing tools like

45
00:01:56,713 --> 00:01:59,934
that requires root access. And for getting

46
00:01:59,998 --> 00:02:02,866
that access, you can add sudo in front of

47
00:02:02,866 --> 00:02:05,205
the command you want to execute. And since

48
00:02:05,205 --> 00:02:07,817
we added the user to the sudo group earlier,

49
00:02:08,234 --> 00:02:11,022
this now will prompt us for the password of

50
00:02:11,054 --> 00:02:13,104
that user. And if you enter that, it will

51
00:02:13,152 --> 00:02:15,348
now run the command with elevated

52
00:02:15,460 --> 00:02:18,187
privileges, so with root user access,

53
00:02:18,412 --> 00:02:20,726
temporary root user access. That is

54
00:02:20,758 --> 00:02:22,702
important. You still needed that password.

55
00:02:23,071 --> 00:02:24,372
That's the security mechanism.

56
00:02:25,031 --> 00:02:27,071
So here, however, it didn't install anything

57
00:02:27,103 --> 00:02:28,662
because I already had UFW.

58
00:02:29,433 --> 00:02:29,545
Now

59
00:02:30,139 --> 00:02:33,288
UFW stands for Uncomplicated Firewall, and

60
00:02:33,288 --> 00:02:35,344
that is an appropriate name because it is

61
00:02:35,521 --> 00:02:36,758
pretty straightforward to use.

62
00:02:37,609 --> 00:02:39,730
For example, you want to use it to allow

63
00:02:40,340 --> 00:02:41,336
OpenSSH,

64
00:02:41,883 --> 00:02:43,618
which simply allows SSH

65
00:02:44,356 --> 00:02:47,467
access to this server. Again, we need root

66
00:02:47,643 --> 00:02:50,289
user access to run this command, so we wanna

67
00:02:50,386 --> 00:02:51,749
prepend it with sudo.

68
00:02:52,951 --> 00:02:56,094
With that done, you wanna run sudo ufw

69
00:02:56,704 --> 00:02:58,612
enable. By the way, instead of always using

70
00:02:58,660 --> 00:03:00,696
sudo here, you could of course also switch

71
00:03:00,809 --> 00:03:03,102
to that terminal where you are connected as

72
00:03:03,150 --> 00:03:05,106
a root user and run the same commands

73
00:03:05,138 --> 00:03:06,261
without sudo here.

74
00:03:06,902 --> 00:03:09,372
But since knowing the concept of sudo is

75
00:03:09,372 --> 00:03:10,879
important, I'll keep on doing this.

76
00:03:12,113 --> 00:03:14,581
Now, if you run this, this firewall will be

77
00:03:14,581 --> 00:03:17,529
enabled, and now you must make sure that you

78
00:03:17,721 --> 00:03:21,102
did allow that SSH connection with that

79
00:03:21,150 --> 00:03:23,586
command I showed you before, with ufw allow

80
00:03:23,794 --> 00:03:26,774
open ssh. If you did not add that rule and

81
00:03:26,774 --> 00:03:29,306
you enable the firewall now, it will not

82
00:03:29,418 --> 00:03:32,094
allow SSH traffic and it will cut your

83
00:03:32,142 --> 00:03:33,712
connection, and there is no way of

84
00:03:33,744 --> 00:03:35,811
connecting back. You would have to reset

85
00:03:35,811 --> 00:03:38,279
your server or shut it down, buy a new one,

86
00:03:38,936 --> 00:03:42,068
You can lock yourself out here if you're not

87
00:03:42,148 --> 00:03:45,585
allowing SSH connections. Make sure you ran

88
00:03:45,698 --> 00:03:47,239
this command here first.

89
00:03:48,091 --> 00:03:49,857
If you did that, you can now enable,

90
00:03:50,516 --> 00:03:52,459
enter Y and hit Enter to do so.

91
00:03:53,246 --> 00:03:54,948
And now it's active and I'm still connected.

92
00:03:55,253 --> 00:03:56,956
That's all looking good. And you can check

93
00:03:57,020 --> 00:03:59,831
that everything is fine by running sudo ufw

94
00:04:00,650 --> 00:04:01,388
status,

95
00:04:02,818 --> 00:04:05,243
status verbose to get even more information.

96
00:04:05,291 --> 00:04:07,138
And with that, you get a nice table, a nice

97
00:04:07,202 --> 00:04:09,322
overview where you see that

98
00:04:10,109 --> 00:04:13,144
SSH traffic is allowed. That is the port 22,

99
00:04:13,224 --> 00:04:15,536
which is the default SSH port. And so any

100
00:04:15,793 --> 00:04:18,185
traffic on port 22 is allowed and that is

101
00:04:18,298 --> 00:04:21,252
the SSH port, typically the SSH traffic,

102
00:04:21,333 --> 00:04:22,681
that's the port reserved for that.

103
00:04:23,340 --> 00:04:25,010
And we're allowing incoming traffic.

104
00:04:25,925 --> 00:04:27,852
No other traffic is allowed though.

105
00:04:28,414 --> 00:04:31,031
So if I were to grab my IP and enter that in

106
00:04:31,063 --> 00:04:31,609
the browser,

107
00:04:32,428 --> 00:04:35,222
nothing would happen because if I try to

108
00:04:35,238 --> 00:04:36,988
visit a webpage like this in the browser,

109
00:04:37,084 --> 00:04:41,372
the browser will by default use port 443 for

110
00:04:41,966 --> 00:04:46,526
HTTPS traffic or port 80 for HTTP unsecured

111
00:04:46,622 --> 00:04:48,999
traffic, but both is not allowed here.

112
00:04:49,689 --> 00:04:51,905
That of course also means that if we were to

113
00:04:52,002 --> 00:04:54,250
run something else on that server, like a

114
00:04:54,266 --> 00:04:56,466
web server that should be reachable via the

115
00:04:56,498 --> 00:04:58,826
World Wide Web, we have to open up that

116
00:04:58,826 --> 00:05:00,592
respective port as well. But that's

117
00:05:00,592 --> 00:05:02,439
something I'll get back to later in the

118
00:05:02,439 --> 00:05:04,334
course when I show you some concrete

119
00:05:04,366 --> 00:05:06,839
examples for what you can do on such a VPS.

120
00:05:07,579 --> 00:05:08,998
But for the moment, we're done setting up

121
00:05:09,062 --> 00:05:10,336
the UFW firewall.

122
00:05:11,206 --> 00:05:13,270
As mentioned though, you might wanna

123
00:05:13,318 --> 00:05:15,495
consider using such a provider firewall.
